course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 24
title: Snowmen, woolly hats, draughts — today and tomorrow
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When it snows, Sashko builds a snIhovyk, a snowman, and shouts "hraimo v snizhky!", let's have a snowball fight!
explain: But first his granny blocks the door: "odYahnysya!" — put your coat on! — and she won't let anyone out "bez shapky", without a woolly hat, even on a mild day.
explain: Ukrainian grannies also fear the protYah, a draught, and shut every window, believing it "will make you ill"; doctors say colds come from germs, not from moving air, but you just smile and close the window.
explain: Then time words: sohOdni (today), zAvtra (tomorrow), vchOra (yesterday), potIm (later), and the tricky "zaraz", which can mean "right now" or "in a minute" — when mum says "zaraz", you wait.
fact: "snIhovyk" means snowman, and "snizhky" means snowballs or a snowball fight, as in "hraimo v snizhky!".
fact: "odYahnysya!" means "put your coat on! dress up warm!", said by the friend's mum or granny at the door.
fact: "shapka" means a woolly hat; "bez shapky", without a hat, worries grannies even in mild weather.
fact: "protYah" means a draught, and many Ukrainian grannies close the windows because they believe a draught makes you ill.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses, not by a draught, but it is polite to respect the granny's worry.
fact: "sohOdni" means today, as in "sohOdni hraiemo?", are we playing today?
fact: "zAvtra" means tomorrow, and "vchOra" means yesterday.
fact: "zaraz" means "now, right now" and also "in a minute"; "zaraz!" can mean "coming!".
fact: "potIm" means later or afterwards, and kids use it to put things off.
fact: "ranok", "den", "vechir" and "nich" mean morning, day, evening and night.
fact: "kotra hodyna?" means "what time is it?", and the answer is "pershA" (one o'clock), "druha" (two) and so on.
exercise: Sashko rolls a big snowball and adds a carrot nose. What is he building? | snIhovyk, a snowman | snizhky, a snowball fight | shapka, a hat | kalyuzha, a puddle
exercise: Sashko shouts "hraimo v snizhky!". What does he want? | A snowball fight | To build a snowman | To go sledging | To stay inside
exercise: Sashko's granny says "odYahnysya!" at the door. What should you do? | Put your coat on | Take your coat off | Go inside | Sit down to eat
exercise: What is a "shapka"? | A woolly hat | A long woolly scarf | A glove | A coat
exercise: Why does Sashko's granny close the window? | She fears a protYah, a draught | She is scared of birds | It is too noisy | She wants it darker
exercise: Sashko asks "sohOdni hraiemo?". When does he want to play? | Today | Tomorrow | Yesterday | Later on
exercise: Sashko says "zAvtra kontrOlna". When is the test? | Tomorrow | Today, after lunch | Yesterday | Next year
exercise: Sashko tells you what happened "vchOra". When did it happen? | Yesterday | Today, this morning | Tomorrow morning | Later this evening
exercise: You ask Sashko's mum for a snack and she says "zaraz". What should you do? | Wait a moment | Go away, it's a no | Get it yourself | Ask again loudly
exercise: Sashko's mum calls him and he shouts "zaraz!". What does he mean? | Coming! | No! | Help! | Later tomorrow!
exercise: Sashko's mum says "pomyi ruky" and he answers "potIm". What does he mean? | Later | Never | Right now | Tomorrow
exercise: Which words mean morning, day, evening and night? | ranok, den, vechir, nich | sohOdni, zAvtra, vchOra, potIm | lito, zyma, vesna, osin | pershA, druha, tretya, chetverta
exercise: You want to know the time. What do you ask? | kotra hodyna? | yaka pohOda? | SKILky tobI rokIv? | de ty zhyvEsh?
exercise: You ask "kotra hodyna?" and Sashko's dad says "pershA". What time is it? | One o'clock | Two o'clock | First lesson | Midnight
test: my mate says when Ukrainian mums say "zaraz" it always means right this second. right? | No, zaraz can also mean "in a minute", so wait | Yes, always this second | No, it always means tomorrow | Yes, and it means hurry up
test: Sashko's granny says a draught gave him a cold. What is the scientific truth? | Colds come from viruses, not from draughts | Draughts always cause colds | Cold air turns into germs | Open windows cause flu every time
test: It's a mild sunny day and you go out without a shapka. What will Sashko's granny most likely do? | Worry and tell you to put one on | Praise you for being brave and tough | Not notice at all | Take her own hat off too
test: Sashko says "vchOra" he got a 12 and "zAvtra" he has football. Which is in the future? | Football | The 12 in the test | Both of them | Neither of them
test: Your mate says "potIm" means "now". What does it really mean? | Later | Right now | Never ever | Yesterday
test: ugh, mum says "odYahnysya" and it's only like 15 degrees. What do you do at Sashko's house? | Smile and put your coat on | Shout "ne mozhna!" back | Ignore her and run | Say "zakryi rota"
test: In a game, a character says "DObroi NOchi" in the "ranok". What went wrong? | He said good night in the morning | He said good morning at night | Nothing, ranok is night | He said hello instead of goodbye
test: Sashko's granny shuts the window, saying "protYah!". What is the polite thing to do? | Let her, it's her house | Open it again behind her | Tell her she's wrong and laugh | Turn on the fan
test: What would happen if you asked "kotra hodyna?" to Sashko's dad? | He'd tell you the time | He'd tell you his age | He'd tell you the weather | He'd show you the toilet
test: Sashko writes the plan: "sohOdni snIhovyk, zAvtra snizhky". What happens tomorrow? | A snowball fight | Building a snowman | Nothing | A test
test: It is getting dark and Sashko's mum says "vzhe vechir, DOdomu". What part of the day is it? | Evening | Morning | Midday | Dawn
exam: Ascertain the action required of a guest when the grandmother of the household pronounces "odYahnysya!" at the threshold. | The donning of outer garments | The removal of outer garments | The removal of footwear | The washing of the hands
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "zaraz" as employed by a Ukrainian parent? | It may signify either "at once" or "in a moment", and the child should therefore wait | It invariably signifies a refusal | It invariably signifies "tomorrow" | It signifies "never" and closes the matter
exam: It is given that a grandmother attributes a common cold to a "protYah". Select the response consistent with the established scientific facts. | Common colds are caused by viruses rather than by currents of air | Currents of air are the sole cause of common colds | Common colds are caused by the wearing of hats | Common colds are caused by snow
exam: Identify the enquiry by which one seeks to ascertain the hour. | kotra hodyna? | yaka pohOda? | yak teBE zvut? | shcho stAlosya?
